And he was hired by Yale

276
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:03,400
as a tutor in mathematics in 1866.

277
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:08,400
And became interested in economics and sociology.

278
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:10,900
He has the distinction of being the first

279
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:13,400
American professor to teach a course called sociology.

280
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:16,900
And in those days, there wasn't the distinction

281
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:19,400
between the social sciences that there is now.

282
00:13:19,400 --> 00:13:22,400
So, he could teach both sociology and economics.

283
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:26,900
So, in his book, I think he started a

284
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:31,400
Yale tradition of conservative economics that lasted until the 1940s.

285
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:33,400
And then Yale kind of

286
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:35,400
drifted more toward the liberal end.

287
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:41,400
But he writes anyway in 1883,

288
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:43,400
Is it wicked to be rich?

289
00:13:44,400 --> 00:13:49,400
Is it mean to be a capitalism, a capitalist?

290
00:13:49,400 --> 00:13:53,900
And he says, first of all, it seems, you

291
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:58,400
know, if capitalists are just richer than other people.

292
00:13:58,400 --> 00:14:00,900
Yes, where is the drawing

293
00:14:00,900 --> 00:14:03,400
dividing line when someone is rich?

294
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:05,400
So, is it wicked to be above that line?

295
00:14:05,400 --> 00:14:07,400
But where do you draw the line?

296
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:10,400
And if you learn from Unger, maybe we're all wicked.

297
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:11,900
Because anyone who doesn't write a

298
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:13,400
$100 check every day to UNICEF,

299
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:18,400
when children are dying around the world, is wicked.

300
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:26,400
But what Sumner is saying, you know, it seems to

301
00:14:26,400 --> 00:14:34,400
be a theme that survives the centuries in favor of capitalists.

302
00:14:34,400 --> 00:14:36,400
I'll quote Sumner,

303
00:14:36,400 --> 00:14:38,400
The great gains of a

304
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:40,400
great capitalist in a modern state

305
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:44,400
must be put under the head of wages of superintendents.

306
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:47,400
Anyone who believes that any

307
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:50,400
great enterprise of an industrial character

308
00:14:50,400 --> 00:14:52,900
can be started without labor

309
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:55,400
must have little experience of life.

310
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:57,400
Let anyone try to get a

311
00:14:57,400 --> 00:14:59,400
railroad built or to start a factory

312
00:14:59,400 --> 00:15:02,400
and win reputation for its products.

313
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:05,400
And he will find what obstacles must be overcome,

314
00:15:05,400 --> 00:15:07,400
what risks must be taken,

315
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:10,400
what perseverance and courage are necessary.

316
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:13,400
So,

317
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:17,400
I don't know that I entirely agree with Sumner.

318
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:22,400
But he has a point that

319
00:15:22,400 --> 00:15:25,400
part of our morality

320
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:27,900
is to do good for the world

321
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:30,400
by doing things like set up railroads

322
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:34,400
or Microsoft.

323
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:38,400
And the kind of activities that that entails will

324
00:15:38,400 --> 00:15:42,400
create opportunities for conspicuous consumption.

325
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:43,400
But not for the people.

326
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:47,900
And I don't necessarily make that the

327
00:15:47,900 --> 00:15:51,400
defining characteristic of someone who does it.

328
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:53,400
Nobody is perfect.

329
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:56,400
And it's hard to judge people ultimately.

330
00:15:56,400 --> 00:15:59,900
But it seems to me that

331
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:03,400
there's almost a moral imperative to entrepreneurship.

332
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:06,400
So,

333
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:07,900
let me go on to the second

334
00:16:07,900 --> 00:16:09,400
theme that I said I would talk about.

335
00:16:09,400 --> 00:16:12,400
And that is hopelessness.

336
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:14,900
A lot of people from

337
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:17,400
their education get the idea that

338
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:21,400
ultimately, there's nothing we can really do.

339
00:16:21,400 --> 00:16:26,400
This is one of Unger's talks about

340
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:31,400
rationalizations we give for not being moral.

341
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:34,400
And he calls it futility is a rationalization.

342
00:16:34,400 --> 00:16:36,400
Ultimately, there's always going to

343
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:38,400
be starving people in the world.

344
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:40,400
And, you know, I can try to help this child,

345
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:41,400
but something is going to get him later.

346
00:16:41,400 --> 00:16:43,400
And so, there's no point.

347
00:16:43,400 --> 00:16:48,400
If you have that kind of futility, sense of futility,

348
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:52,400
it can justify any amount of hedonism.

349
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:57,400
But I think that the classic article that

350
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:02,400
lends most people to that sense of futility,

351
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:05,400
and I assume you know about this already,

352
00:17:05,400 --> 00:17:10,400
but it's Malthus, 1798.

353
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:14,400
His essay on, well, the title of it was

354
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:18,400
Essay on the Principle of Population.

355
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:24,400
So, Thomas Malthus wrote about the population problem.

356
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:26,900
It was such a celebrated essay that

357
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:29,400
he went through six editions of it.

358
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:31,900
But I'm going to quote from

359
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:34,400
the first edition just to remind you.

360
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:37,400
He said in 1798,

361
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:40,400
Population, when unchecked,

362
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:43,400
increased in a geometrical ratio

363
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:48,400
and subsistence for man in an arithmetical ratio.

364
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:51,400
You know, so the population

365
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:54,400
growth follows an exponential growth curve.

366
00:17:54,400 --> 00:17:59,400
He says geometrical, but it goes like that.

367
00:17:59,400 --> 00:18:01,400
Whereas he said, at best,

368
00:18:01,400 --> 00:18:05,400
the increase in our ability to produce is linear,

369
00:18:05,400 --> 00:18:07,400
or he calls that arithmetical.

370
00:18:07,400 --> 00:18:08,400
And so,

371
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:15,400
population will run off with all of our resources.

372
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:17,400
There's nothing we can do.

373
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:21,400
Population will continue to put pressure on our resources.

374
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:28,400
He says, I'm quoting again from his 1798 essay,

375
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:31,400
Population, when unchecked, goes on

376
00:18:31,400 --> 00:18:34,400
doubling itself every 25 years.

377
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:37,400
In the next, if you go through two 25-year periods,

378
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:40,400
where there was no check on population growth,

379
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:42,400
it is impossible, I'm quoting him,

380
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:45,400
to suppose that the produce could be quadrupled.

381
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:48,400
It would be contrary to all

382
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:51,400
our knowledge of the qualities of land.

383
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:59,400
Then he comes now to his dismal law of economics.

384
00:18:59,400 --> 00:19:00,400
He didn't call it that.

385
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:03,400
I'm quoting, but I will quote him.

386
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:06,400
No possible form of society

387
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:10,400
could prevent the almost constant action of misery

388
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:12,400
upon a great part of mankind,

389
00:19:12,400 --> 00:19:15,400
if in a state of inequality,

390
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:20,400
and upon all, if all were equal.

391
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:23,400
So, that the natural state of humankind

392
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:27,400
is bordering on starvation and dying.

393
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:30,400
And the force of his argument was quite profound,

394
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:33,400
because it was hard to argue against him

395
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:35,400
that there's nothing you can do about it.

396
00:19:35,400 --> 00:19:37,400
That's just it.

397
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:40,400
And all the theorizing of people

398
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:42,400
can only result in a world

399
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:46,400
where more people are suffering and dying.

400
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:49,400
If you want to think more about this,

401
00:19:49,400 --> 00:19:54,400
I suggest you might go to Robert Wyman,

402
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:57,400
who is a professor here at Yale,

403
00:19:57,400 --> 00:20:01,400
on Open Yale,

404
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:03,400
which means it's another of these

405
00:20:03,400 --> 00:20:07,400
courses open on the Internet.

406
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:09,400
Has a course called

407
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:12,400
Global Problems of Population Growth,

408
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:14,400
where he spends a whole semester

409
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:19,400
thinking about the Malthusian problem.

410
00:20:19,400 --> 00:20:21,400
One thing that he talks about in that course

411
00:20:21,400 --> 00:20:24,400
is that there's a popular sense that

412
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:27,400
the population problem is not so bad anymore,

413
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:29,400
because many countries have

414
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:31,400
introduced birth control policies.

415
00:20:31,400 --> 00:20:36,400
I mean, notably China has a one-child policy, supposedly.

416
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:38,400
It's not really a one-child policy.

417
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:40,400
It's not enforced that well.

418
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:43,400
It's more like a two-child policy,

419
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:46,400
and people have even more than that.

420
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:48,400
But other countries, India,

421
00:20:48,400 --> 00:20:50,400
and certain regions of India have made great progress,

422
00:20:50,400 --> 00:20:53,400
we're told, in birth control.

423
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:55,400
But still, despite that,

424
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:57,900
Wyman estimates that the world adds

425
00:20:57,900 --> 00:21:00,400
a billion people every 12 years.

426
00:21:01,400 --> 00:21:04,400
So, that's a problem.

427
00:21:04,400 --> 00:21:07,400
And our resources are limited.

428
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:09,400
So, the problem is that

429
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:11,400
people are crowding into the cities,

430
00:21:11,400 --> 00:21:14,400
because there's no room for them on the land.

431
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,400
They're trying to get an education

432
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,400
to push themselves ahead,

433
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:20,400
but the sheer numbers of people

434
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:24,400
make it impossible for them all to get ahead.

435
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:29,400
So, I suggest that you might take his course.

436
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:31,400
It's a problem that people don't want

437
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:33,400
to get up to.

438
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:35,400
So, I'm sounding very dismal here.

439
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:38,400
But actually, I think that the problem

440
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:44,400
is not as bad as it may seem.

441
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:46,400
This is my take on it.

442
00:21:46,400 --> 00:21:49,400
I tend to be a realist about these things.

443
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:51,400
We have a population problem.

444
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:52,400
It's going to be with us.

445
00:21:52,400 --> 00:21:55,400
But hey, it's been with us forever,

446
00:21:55,400 --> 00:21:58,400
going all the way back in history.

447
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:01,400
So, it's a tough world that we live in,

448
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:06,400
where the human race is naturally procreating

449
00:22:06,400 --> 00:22:09,400
and naturally creating population pressures

450
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:14,400
and conflicts that lead to wars and famines.

451
00:22:14,400 --> 00:22:17,400
We've been going through a good run

452
00:22:17,400 --> 00:22:19,400
in the last few centuries,

453
00:22:19,400 --> 00:22:21,400
but it's growing.

454
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:22,400
I don't know when the end,

455
00:22:22,400 --> 00:22:23,400
it's not an end,

456
00:22:23,400 --> 00:22:29,400
when we're going to see more severe problems.

457
00:22:29,400 --> 00:22:33,400
That just seems to be right and inevitable.

458
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:35,400
But I think that that doesn't mean,

459
00:22:35,400 --> 00:22:39,400
I think that the weakest part of Malthus' argument

460
00:22:39,400 --> 00:22:42,400
is the last step, saying that it's necessarily

461
00:22:42,400 --> 00:22:46,400
a dismal world that results.

462
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:48,400
I wanted to put the

463
00:22:48,400 --> 00:22:50,400
bright side on Malthus' dismal law.

464
00:22:50,400 --> 00:22:53,400
And that is, most of the time, everyone's fine.

465
00:22:53,400 --> 00:22:56,400
In the world, most people, you know,

466
00:22:56,400 --> 00:22:59,400
it's famines and wars are intermittent events.

467
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:01,400
That reduce population.

468
00:23:01,400 --> 00:23:03,400
Between those big events,

469
00:23:03,400 --> 00:23:06,400
pretty much everyone is doing all right.

470
00:23:06,400 --> 00:23:07,400
So, I mean, maybe it's

471
00:23:07,400 --> 00:23:08,400
not as bad as you think.

472
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:11,400
You know, you might get killed in a war someday,

473
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:14,400
but you enjoy life until that happens.

474
00:23:14,400 --> 00:23:18,400
I mean, that's very basic, but I think it's true.

475
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:19,900
But moreover, I think that there's

476
00:23:19,900 --> 00:23:21,400
a lot that we can do

477
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:24,400
to make life better,

478
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:28,400
even in the context of the dismal law,

479
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:30,400
and accepting the dismal law.

480
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:34,400
And I think that civilization is improving,

481
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:36,400
so that life is better,

482
00:23:36,400 --> 00:23:39,400
even though there are population pressures.

483
00:23:39,400 --> 00:23:41,400
And that's why I think there's a, you know,

484
00:23:41,400 --> 00:23:43,400
maybe I'm saying the obvious here,

485
00:23:43,400 --> 00:23:45,400
but I want to say it anyway,

486
00:23:45,400 --> 00:23:47,400
that there are plenty of purposes and goals

487
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:52,400
that people can fulfill,

488
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:56,400
even taking as given Malthus' dismal law.

489
00:23:56,400 --> 00:23:58,400
So, I talked last period,

490
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:02,400
about nonprofit and charity,

491
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:06,400
and government as well.

492
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:08,400
There are a lot of

493
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:10,400
people who are doing specific things

494
00:24:10,400 --> 00:24:13,400
to make the world respond better

495
00:24:13,400 --> 00:24:18,400
to the dismal law of Malthus.

496
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:24,400
And I wanted to mention certain examples,

497
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:26,400
just to make this clear.

498
00:24:26,400 --> 00:24:28,400
I think that there's work being done

499
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:30,400
by governments of the world.

500
00:24:30,400 --> 00:24:33,400
There's also work being done by individuals

501
00:24:33,400 --> 00:24:36,400
who don't need government.

502
00:24:36,400 --> 00:24:38,400
They set up their own organizations.

503
00:24:38,400 --> 00:24:44,400
So, I was specifically talking about the environment.

504
00:24:44,400 --> 00:24:48,400
This is what's being threatened by population growth.

505
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:50,400
And so, there are many foundations

506
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:55,400
that deal with the environment.

507
00:24:55,400 --> 00:24:56,400
I'll just mention a few.

508
00:24:56,400 --> 00:24:58,400
The Nature Conservancy,

509
00:24:58,400 --> 00:25:00,400
the World Wildlife Fund,

510
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:04,400
the Wildlife Conservation Society,

511
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:06,400
and there's specialized ones called,

512
00:25:06,400 --> 00:25:08,400
like African Wildlife Foundation,

513
00:25:08,400 --> 00:25:11,400
the Jane Goodall Institute,

514
00:25:11,400 --> 00:25:14,400
the Dian Fossey Gorilla Fund.

515
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:16,400
You know, you think the gorillas out there are wild,

516
00:25:16,400 --> 00:25:18,400
but there's finance and support.

517
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:19,400
They've collected money.

518
00:25:19,400 --> 00:25:22,400
Someone is managing an endowment for the gorillas.

519
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:23,400
Okay?

520
00:25:23,400 --> 00:25:26,400
This is creative finance.

521
00:25:26,400 --> 00:25:29,400
You know, a part of the problem with,

522
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:30,400
you think about what's happening

523
00:25:30,400 --> 00:25:33,400
with the population pressures of the world.

524
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:36,400
And is it bad or not?

525
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:38,400
Well, in some sense, it's good.

526
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:40,400
Having, you know, 10 billion people on the world

527
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:43,400
would just make it for a more interesting place, right?

528
00:25:43,400 --> 00:25:45,400
There'd be more arts and sciences

529
00:25:45,400 --> 00:25:47,400
and fun things to do.

530
00:25:47,400 --> 00:25:50,400
Eventually, we're going to colonize the moon and Mars,

531
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:52,400
and there's going to be fun trips to do.

532
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:54,400
So, I don't know if it's a bad world

533
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:55,400
that we're coming into.

534
00:25:55,400 --> 00:25:57,400
Even if there are conflicts.

535
00:25:57,400 --> 00:25:59,400
But part of, you know,

536
00:25:59,400 --> 00:26:01,400
I think there are specific problems

537
00:26:01,400 --> 00:26:02,400
with that world.

538
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:05,400
And one of them is the extinction of species.

539
00:26:05,400 --> 00:26:06,400
You think about that.

540
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:09,400
You know, we're destroying habitat for species,

541
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:11,400
and they're going to be gone forever.

542
00:26:11,400 --> 00:26:16,400
So, but the thing is that those kinds of problems

543
00:26:16,400 --> 00:26:20,400
are problems that have sort of business solutions.

544
00:26:20,400 --> 00:26:23,400
And I wanted to talk about one particular foundation.

545
00:26:23,400 --> 00:26:24,400
I don't know if you've heard of it.

546
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:26,400
It's a non-profit.

547
00:26:26,400 --> 00:26:29,400
And I'll just give this as an example.

548
00:26:29,400 --> 00:26:32,400
The Nature Conservancy.

549
00:26:32,400 --> 00:26:39,400
It was founded in 1951 in the United States,

550
00:26:39,400 --> 00:26:42,400
but it now operates in 30 countries.

551
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:49,400
Its total assets are 5.6 billion,

552
00:26:49,400 --> 00:26:52,400
which makes it the third largest charity

553
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:53,400
in the United States.

554
00:26:54,400 --> 00:27:08,400
And they have a principle of conservation by design.

555
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:10,900
The idea is, our purpose

556
00:27:10,900 --> 00:27:13,400
is to prevent extinction of species.

557
00:27:13,400 --> 00:27:15,400
Because extinction is forever.

558
00:27:15,400 --> 00:27:17,400
You know, these species have taken hundreds of millions

559
00:27:17,400 --> 00:27:19,400
of years to evolve.

560
00:27:19,400 --> 00:27:21,400
They get wiped out, they're gone.

561
00:27:21,400 --> 00:27:23,400
As far as we know, they're gone forever.

562
00:27:24,400 --> 00:27:29,400
What they do is, they get scientists who specialize

563
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:33,400
in environment and biology, and they say,

564
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:37,400
which species are endangered, and what can we really do

565
00:27:37,400 --> 00:27:40,400
to prevent their extinction?

566
00:27:40,400 --> 00:27:43,400
And one thing that the scientists have been telling them

567
00:27:43,400 --> 00:27:47,400
is that you have to preserve habitat for species,

568
00:27:47,400 --> 00:27:51,400
and you have to do it with purpose and clarity.

569
00:27:51,400 --> 00:27:53,400
What do these animals need?

570
00:27:53,400 --> 00:27:55,400
Some of them are migratory, for example,

571
00:27:55,400 --> 00:27:57,400
and they migrate over long distances.

572
00:27:57,400 --> 00:27:59,400
So, you have to preserve a migratory route

573
00:27:59,400 --> 00:28:02,400
and stopping places along the way for them.

574
00:28:02,400 --> 00:28:05,400
So, it has to be done well.

575
00:28:05,400 --> 00:28:09,400
So, the Nature Conservancy believes also that the way

576
00:28:09,400 --> 00:28:12,400
to protect habitat is to buy

577
00:28:12,400 --> 00:28:15,400
land and put up fences around,

578
00:28:15,400 --> 00:28:17,400
have to keep people out,

579
00:28:17,400 --> 00:28:19,400
and then have a forest manager

580
00:28:19,400 --> 00:28:20,400
run it, so that the

581
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:21,400
species will have a good life.

582
00:28:21,400 --> 00:28:22,400
So, they have to do it.

583
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:30,400
And they say that they have 500,000 square kilometers

584
00:28:30,400 --> 00:28:34,400
of land that they've bought around the world.

585
00:28:34,400 --> 00:28:38,400
I calculated that there's 150 million square kilometers

586
00:28:38,400 --> 00:28:40,400
of land on the Earth.

587
00:28:40,400 --> 00:28:42,900
So, they have one-third of

588
00:28:42,900 --> 00:28:45,400
1% of the world's land protected

589
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:48,400
by their charity.

590
00:28:48,400 --> 00:28:49,400
Now, that might seem small,

591
00:28:49,400 --> 00:28:50,400
but that's a big difference, right?

592
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:51,400
Because if it's the last thing they have,

593
00:28:51,400 --> 00:28:52,400
that's a big difference.

594
00:28:52,400 --> 00:28:54,400
Because if it's the last habitat, that might be enough

595
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:57,400
to keep a lot of species diversity going.

596
00:28:57,400 --> 00:29:05,400
So, that's an example of what, this is really finance.

597
00:29:05,400 --> 00:29:10,400
We have portfolio managers managing portfolio

598
00:29:10,400 --> 00:29:16,400
and properties of land with a good purpose.

599
00:29:21,400 --> 00:29:26,400
And, you know, that's why I say the moral dilemmas

600
00:29:26,400 --> 00:29:27,400
are not so simple.

601
00:29:27,400 --> 00:29:29,400
You could take a job managing a portfolio for one

602
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:32,400
of these foundations.

603
00:29:32,400 --> 00:29:38,400
Peter Unger, in his book, is talking about, always about,

604
00:29:38,400 --> 00:29:39,400
you know, would you save a

605
00:29:39,400 --> 00:29:40,400
child who fell in the water

606
00:29:40,400 --> 00:29:42,400
or something like that.

607
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:45,900
But that's not the kind of

608
00:29:45,900 --> 00:29:49,400
moral dilemmas that we really face.

609
00:29:49,400 --> 00:29:52,400
That an energetic intellect would find.

610
00:29:52,400 --> 00:29:54,900
The moral dilemma is to

611
00:29:54,900 --> 00:29:57,400
prevent big, bad things from happening.

612
00:29:57,400 --> 00:30:00,400
And that takes a sort of entrepreneurship and big

613
00:30:00,400 --> 00:30:04,400
thinking to manage.

614
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:08,400
Another thought I had in this context is about wars.

615
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:10,400
I was saying that population

616
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:12,400
pressures are a fact of life.

617
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:18,400
And I'm skeptical that anyone will change the basic

618
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:21,400
nature of the situation.

619
00:30:21,400 --> 00:30:28,400
But I wanted in this context to emphasize that financial

620
00:30:28,400 --> 00:30:36,400
arrangements are capable of enduring and surviving wars

621
00:30:36,400 --> 00:30:38,400
and catastrophes.

622
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:41,400
I wanted particularly to make

623
00:30:41,400 --> 00:30:44,400
it clear that there's a tendency

624
00:30:44,400 --> 00:30:45,900
for people to think that

625
00:30:45,900 --> 00:30:47,400
finance is something that the government

626
00:30:47,400 --> 00:30:48,400
