00:39:42,400 --> 00:39:45,900
And there's also this sense

820
00:39:45,900 --> 00:39:49,400
that we're living in a world

821
00:39:49,400 --> 00:39:54,400
that's increasingly plutocratic, that the wealthy people are

822
00:39:54,400 --> 00:40:00,400
controlling the world.

823
00:40:00,400 --> 00:40:02,400
That was a theme that took

824
00:40:02,400 --> 00:40:04,400
a lot of impetus in the 19th

825
00:40:04,400 --> 00:40:06,400
century with Karl Marx, who said exactly that.

826
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:08,400
And in some sense, it's coming

827
00:40:08,400 --> 00:40:10,400
back, maybe in not such an

828
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:12,400
extreme form.

829
00:40:12,400 --> 00:40:16,400
So, Jacob Hacker, who's in our political science department,

830
00:40:16,400 --> 00:40:22,400
and Paul Pearson, who's at Stanford University, have a

831
00:40:22,400 --> 00:40:29,400
new book that just came out called, Winner Take All

832
00:40:29,400 --> 00:40:31,400
Politics.

833
00:40:31,400 --> 00:40:35,400
And that book is about politics.

834
00:40:35,400 --> 00:40:37,900
And the book is about, they

835
00:40:37,900 --> 00:40:40,400
have a claim in that book that

836
00:40:40,400 --> 00:40:45,400
the world is getting more polarized by the political

837
00:40:45,400 --> 00:40:49,400
power of financial institutions.

838
00:40:49,400 --> 00:40:52,400
Basically, they have something they call the 30 Years War.

839
00:40:52,400 --> 00:40:53,400
What's the 30 Years War?

840
00:40:53,400 --> 00:40:56,400
You might think it's something that happened in the 17th

841
00:40:56,400 --> 00:40:57,400
century.

842
00:40:57,400 --> 00:40:58,400
Not for them.

843
00:40:58,400 --> 00:40:59,900
The 30 Years War is the

844
00:40:59,900 --> 00:41:01,400
war against the people of the world

845
00:41:01,400 --> 00:41:04,400
fought by the financial community.

846
00:41:04,400 --> 00:41:05,900
And the 30 Years War is the

847
00:41:05,900 --> 00:41:07,400
war against the people of the world

848
00:41:07,400 --> 00:41:11,400
fought by the halls of Congress and Parliament, by lobbying.

849
00:41:11,400 --> 00:41:15,400
So, they argue that the companies have gotten more and

850
00:41:15,400 --> 00:41:20,400
more sophisticated in lobbying governments to fulfill their

851
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:22,400
ambitions.

852
00:41:22,400 --> 00:41:26,400
And so, the income inequality that we're seeing increasing,

853
00:41:26,400 --> 00:41:29,400
particularly in the United States, but elsewhere in the

854
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:32,400
world, is a consequence of this.

855
00:41:32,400 --> 00:41:34,900
And so, much of the literature

856
00:41:34,900 --> 00:41:37,400
on income inequality says it has

857
00:41:37,400 --> 00:41:40,400
something to do with the information revolution, which is

858
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:46,400
eliminating jobs for low-income people, and the increasing

859
00:41:46,400 --> 00:41:48,400
importance of education, which

860
00:41:48,400 --> 00:41:50,400
rewards college graduates at the

861
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:53,400
expense of uneducated people.

862
00:41:53,400 --> 00:41:55,400
But they say that the

863
00:41:55,400 --> 00:41:57,400
real increase in inequality has not

864
00:41:57,400 --> 00:42:01,400
been between high school graduates and college

865
00:42:01,400 --> 00:42:03,400
graduates.

866
00:42:03,400 --> 00:42:06,400
It's between the whole population and the top tenth of

867
00:42:06,400 --> 00:42:08,400
a percent.

868
00:42:08,400 --> 00:42:10,400
There is this small community

869
00:42:10,400 --> 00:42:12,400
of super rich people that are

870
00:42:12,400 --> 00:42:17,400
developing, who are very adept at lobbying governments.

871
00:42:17,400 --> 00:42:19,400
This is a trend that's developing.

872
00:42:19,400 --> 00:42:25,400
So, well, I think, to some extent, they are probably

873
00:42:25,400 --> 00:42:26,400
right.

874
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:27,900
I think maybe they overstate

875
00:42:27,900 --> 00:42:29,400
that, but I think it's a

876
00:42:29,400 --> 00:42:30,400
concern.

877
00:42:30,400 --> 00:42:31,400
But I think it's a concern.

878
00:42:31,400 --> 00:42:35,400
I think that we do have democratic institutions, and

879
00:42:35,400 --> 00:42:40,400
we can respond to that.

880
00:42:40,400 --> 00:42:45,400
I think maybe they overstate it, because I think that

881
00:42:45,400 --> 00:42:47,400
I've met billionaires in my life,

882
00:42:47,400 --> 00:42:49,400
and I have a sense that

883
00:42:49,400 --> 00:42:53,400
they are not, I guess they have, sometimes they might

884
00:42:53,400 --> 00:42:56,400
have, I haven't met enough of them to make generalities

885
00:42:56,400 --> 00:42:57,900
about billionaires, but maybe they

886
00:42:57,900 --> 00:42:59,400
have a little bit of a

887
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:00,400
self-serving mentality.

888
00:43:00,400 --> 00:43:03,400
But in some sense, they seem not to care.

889
00:43:03,400 --> 00:43:08,400
They don't want to be viewed as evil.

890
00:43:08,400 --> 00:43:09,900
They want to be, a lot

891
00:43:09,900 --> 00:43:11,400
of what drove them to become

892
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:13,400
billionaires was a sense that

893
00:43:13,400 --> 00:43:15,400
they would be a benefactor of

894
00:43:15,400 --> 00:43:16,400
some sort.

895
00:43:16,400 --> 00:43:18,400
And so, they're ready to give it away.

896
00:43:18,400 --> 00:43:22,400
Anyway, that may be a casual impression.

897
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:25,400
But one thing that angers people about wealth is the

898
00:43:25,400 --> 00:43:28,400
tendency of wealthy people to build monuments to themselves.

899
00:43:30,400 --> 00:43:32,400
So, I was thinking of that

900
00:43:32,400 --> 00:43:34,400
when I was at the J.P. Morgan

901
00:43:34,400 --> 00:43:38,400
Library in New York.

902
00:43:38,400 --> 00:43:41,400
And also, there's something called the Metropolitan Club,

903
00:43:41,400 --> 00:43:44,400
which is another building that he built to himself.

904
00:43:44,400 --> 00:43:50,400
This huge mansion in Manhattan that he built.

905
00:43:50,400 --> 00:43:53,400
And I was thinking, is J.P. Morgan evil?

906
00:43:53,400 --> 00:43:54,400
I mean, people are starving

907
00:43:54,400 --> 00:43:55,400
in the world, and he's building

908
00:43:55,400 --> 00:43:57,400
a mansion for himself.

909
00:43:57,400 --> 00:43:58,400
But then I reflected further.

910
00:43:58,400 --> 00:43:59,400
Here, I am having dinner in his house.

911
00:43:59,400 --> 00:44:01,400
Here, I am having dinner in his mansion.

912
00:44:01,400 --> 00:44:03,400
He's gone.

913
00:44:03,400 --> 00:44:08,900
And is it really so

914
00:44:08,900 --> 00:44:14,400
bad in the scheme of things?

915
00:44:20,400 --> 00:44:23,400
So, it comes down to how you,

916
00:44:23,400 --> 00:44:26,400
I guess you can view it in

917
00:44:26,400 --> 00:44:27,400
different ways.

918
00:44:27,400 --> 00:44:28,400
You can view J.P. Morgan as

919
00:44:28,400 --> 00:44:29,400
a great success, who was a

920
00:44:29,400 --> 00:44:36,400
great businessman, who ended up helping the world.

921
00:44:36,400 --> 00:44:40,400
Or you can view him as a selfish monument builder.

922
00:44:46,400 --> 00:44:48,400
But so, his life overlapped

923
00:44:48,400 --> 00:44:50,400
with Karl Marx, that I told

924
00:44:50,400 --> 00:44:51,400
you about.

925
00:44:51,400 --> 00:44:54,400
But one of Karl Marx's themes

926
00:44:54,400 --> 00:44:57,400
was that the system is unfair,

927
00:44:57,400 --> 00:44:58,400
that some people have capital.

928
00:44:58,400 --> 00:44:59,400
And I think that's the

929
00:44:59,400 --> 00:45:00,400
essence of his book Das Kapital.

930
00:45:00,400 --> 00:45:01,900
Some people have capital, and

931
00:45:01,900 --> 00:45:03,400
they are wealthy as a result,

932
00:45:03,400 --> 00:45:04,400
and they will continue to

933
00:45:04,400 --> 00:45:05,400
be wealthy, and they'll make their

934
00:45:05,400 --> 00:45:07,400
children wealthy as a result.

935
00:45:07,400 --> 00:45:10,400
I actually have a quote from Karl Marx.

936
00:45:10,400 --> 00:45:12,400
It is not because he is

937
00:45:12,400 --> 00:45:14,400
a leader of industry that a man

938
00:45:14,400 --> 00:45:15,400
is a capitalist.

939
00:45:15,400 --> 00:45:17,400
On the contrary, he is a

940
00:45:17,400 --> 00:45:19,400
leader of industry because he is a

941
00:45:19,400 --> 00:45:20,400
capitalist.

942
00:45:20,400 --> 00:45:24,400
The leadership of industry is an attribute of capital, just

943
00:45:24,400 --> 00:45:27,400
as in feudal times, the functions of capital were not

944
00:45:27,400 --> 00:45:29,400
the same.

945
00:45:29,400 --> 00:45:34,400
The functions of general and judge were attributes of landed

946
00:45:34,400 --> 00:45:35,400
property.

947
00:45:35,400 --> 00:45:42,400
That comes from his book, Kapital, in 19th century.

948
00:45:42,400 --> 00:45:45,900
So, Marx thought that ownership of

949
00:45:45,900 --> 00:45:49,400
capital was like a key to

950
00:45:49,400 --> 00:45:54,400
the good life, and that the population was excluded from

951
00:45:54,400 --> 00:45:55,400
that.

952
00:45:55,400 --> 00:45:57,400
But I'm going to come back to the democratization of

953
00:45:57,400 --> 00:45:58,400
capitalism.

954
00:45:58,400 --> 00:46:01,400
I think capitalism is a very important class in the

955
00:46:01,400 --> 00:46:02,400
world of finance.

956
00:46:02,400 --> 00:46:05,400
But it seems like capitalism, it isn't essential to

957
00:46:05,400 --> 00:46:10,400
capitalism that some social class dominates capital.

958
00:46:10,400 --> 00:46:13,400
We can have a capitalism that

959
00:46:13,400 --> 00:46:16,400
is divided up among, that is

960
00:46:16,400 --> 00:46:19,400
more of people's.

961
00:46:19,400 --> 00:46:25,400
It belongs to people, and it's not a privileged class.

962
00:46:25,400 --> 00:46:26,900
So, I think that's what I'm

963
00:46:26,900 --> 00:46:28,400
going to talk about, is my

964
00:46:28,400 --> 00:46:29,400
concern.

965
00:46:29,400 --> 00:46:30,900
You know, Marx was impressed, I

966
00:46:30,900 --> 00:46:32,400
think I may have said this

967
00:46:32,400 --> 00:46:36,400
before, he was impressive because he read emerging

968
00:46:36,400 --> 00:46:37,400
sociology.

969
00:46:37,400 --> 00:46:39,400
And the sociology of his

970
00:46:39,400 --> 00:46:41,400
day was beginning to recognize that

971
00:46:41,400 --> 00:46:43,400
people do form themselves into

972
00:46:43,400 --> 00:46:45,400
social classes, and they have a

973
00:46:45,400 --> 00:46:48,400
sense of loyalty to others in their social class.

974
00:46:48,400 --> 00:46:49,900
But I think that, you know,

975
00:46:49,900 --> 00:46:51,400
anything he said is of limited

976
00:46:51,400 --> 00:46:53,400
relevance today.

977
00:46:53,400 --> 00:46:55,400
It was always of limited relevance.

978
00:46:55,400 --> 00:46:57,400
And it's wrong in many ways.

979
00:46:57,400 --> 00:47:08,400
But I'm thinking of the works of another important thinker,

980
00:47:08,400 --> 00:47:15,400
Robert K. Merton, who is a sociologist at Columbia.

981
00:47:15,400 --> 00:47:18,900
He is the father of

982
00:47:18,900 --> 00:47:22,400
the Robert Merton economist, who helped

983
00:47:22,400 --> 00:47:24,400
develop option theory.

984
00:47:24,400 --> 00:47:29,400
But Robert K. Merton referred to what he called the

985
00:47:29,400 --> 00:47:32,400
cosmopolitan class.

986
00:47:32,400 --> 00:47:35,400
So, he was looking at social classes.

987
00:47:35,400 --> 00:47:36,900
He picked a small town in

988
00:47:36,900 --> 00:47:38,400
the United States and interviewed a

989
00:47:38,400 --> 00:47:42,400
lot of people, and was trying to understand their class

990
00:47:42,400 --> 00:47:43,400
structure, you know, who do

991
00:47:43,400 --> 00:47:44,400
you identify with, who are you

992
00:47:44,400 --> 00:47:46,400
loyal with.

993
00:47:46,400 --> 00:47:49,900
And he was a deep thinker,

994
00:47:49,900 --> 00:47:53,400
I think, and looked at what

995
00:47:53,400 --> 00:47:56,400
really seemed to separate people.

996
00:47:56,400 --> 00:47:58,400
And he decided that in

997
00:47:58,400 --> 00:48:00,400
this little town, that there were

998
00:48:00,400 --> 00:48:02,400
really two classes of people.

999
00:48:02,400 --> 00:48:08,400
He called them cosmopolitans and locals.

1000
00:48:08,400 --> 00:48:14,400
So, the cosmopolitans had a very different world view.

1001
00:48:14,400 --> 00:48:15,900
They tended to not care about

1002
00:48:15,900 --> 00:48:17,400
what's going on in their town.

1003
00:48:17,400 --> 00:48:20,400
They would talk about national or international things.

1004
00:48:20,400 --> 00:48:23,400
You know, you'd listen to what they say.

1005
00:48:23,400 --> 00:48:25,400
They were focused outside.

1006
00:48:25,400 --> 00:48:27,400
They thought the town was irrelevant.

1007
00:48:27,400 --> 00:48:32,400
And it tended to have maybe higher level positions.

1008
00:48:32,400 --> 00:48:34,400
The locals were people, though,

1009
00:48:34,400 --> 00:48:36,400
who would talk all about their

1010
00:48:36,400 --> 00:48:39,400
town, and they would talk about people they know.

1011
00:48:39,400 --> 00:48:44,400
They seemed to value their connections within the town.

1012
00:48:44,400 --> 00:48:47,400
And when you asked for opinions

1013
00:48:47,400 --> 00:48:50,400
about the local town, the locals

1014
00:48:50,400 --> 00:48:52,400
would tend to give almost loving expressions.

1015
00:48:52,400 --> 00:48:54,400
This is a great town.

1016
00:48:54,400 --> 00:48:56,400
We have great people here.

1017
00:48:56,400 --> 00:48:59,400
And the cosmopolitans would act totally indifferent.

1018
00:48:59,400 --> 00:49:01,400
And they don't know anybody.

1019
00:49:01,400 --> 00:49:02,900
They don't know who's the head

1020
00:49:02,900 --> 00:49:04,400
of the fire department, or who

1021
00:49:04,400 --> 00:49:06,900
holds the, maybe they know

1022
00:49:06,900 --> 00:49:09,400
the principal of the school, because

1023
00:49:09,400 --> 00:49:11,400
they may have their kid in the school.

1024
00:49:11,400 --> 00:49:14,400
But beyond that, they don't know anything about their town.

1025
00:49:14,400 --> 00:49:18,400
So, Merton wrote that over 50 years ago.

1026
00:49:18,400 --> 00:49:21,400
I have a sense that it's developing further, this split.

1027
00:49:21,400 --> 00:49:24,400
Between cosmopolitans and locals.

1028
00:49:24,400 --> 00:49:27,400
And it's developing on a world scale.

1029
00:49:27,400 --> 00:49:31,400
There is now a world cosmopolitan class.

1030
00:49:31,400 --> 00:49:33,900
And with increased communications, we're

1031
00:49:33,900 --> 00:49:36,400
kind of split that way.

1032
00:49:36,400 --> 00:49:38,400
So, people around the world

1033
00:49:38,400 --> 00:49:40,400
who are learning to speak English,

1034
00:49:40,400 --> 00:49:43,400
well, that's the world language.

1035
00:49:43,400 --> 00:49:46,400
People who travel around the world.

1036
00:49:46,400 --> 00:49:50,400
And people who are finance savvy.

1037
00:49:51,400 --> 00:49:54,900
And people who are also

1038
00:49:54,900 --> 00:49:58,400
are developing into a social class.

1039
00:49:58,400 --> 00:50:02,400
And I think that there are animosities and conflicts.

1040
00:50:02,400 --> 00:50:04,400
But it's a little bit

1041
00:50:04,400 --> 00:50:06,400
harder to have, because the cosmopolitans

1042
00:50:06,400 --> 00:50:09,400
are so scattered, and they're relatives of us.

1043
00:50:09,400 --> 00:50:15,400
So, it's not as intense a social contrast.

1044
00:50:15,400 --> 00:50:17,900
But I think that the animosities that we are

1045
00:50:17,900 --> 00:50:20,400
feeling now, have to do with the fact that

1046
00:50:20,400 --> 00:50:24,400
cosmopolitans know and understand finance.

1047
00:50:24,400 --> 00:50:28,400
And they have lawyers and advisors.

1048
00:50:28,400 --> 00:50:33,400
The rest of the population feels excluded from that.

1049
00:50:33,400 --> 00:50:37,900
So, that brings us to what

1050
00:50:37,900 --> 00:50:42,400
I said was the democratization of finance.

1051
00:50:42,400 --> 00:50:44,900
And this is a theme of

1052
00:50:44,900 --> 00:50:47,400
my own, that I've been emphasizing.

1053
00:50:47,400 --> 00:50:48,900
So, the democratization of finance is

1054
00:50:48,900 --> 00:50:50,400
sort of trying to make it

1055
00:50:50,400 --> 00:50:55,400
move beyond the cosmopolitan class.

1056
00:50:55,400 --> 00:50:59,900
So, cosmopolitans know how to get

1057
00:50:59,900 --> 00:51:04,400
things done, how to raise capital.

1058
00:51:04,400 --> 00:51:07,400
And they know how to manage their

1059
00:51:07,400 --> 00:51:10,400
risks, so they don't get into trouble.

1060
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:13,900
Inequality is substantially due to

1061
00:51:13,900 --> 00:51:17,400
a failure to manage risks.

1062
00:51:17,400 --> 00:51:21,400
So, in the, right?

1063
00:51:21,400 --> 00:51:24,400
I mean, some inequality is due to fundamental things,

1064
00:51:24,400 --> 00:51:27,400
like someone is talented and can make more money.

1065
00:51:27,400 --> 00:51:29,900
But it's also due to

1066
00:51:29,900 --> 00:51:32,400
random things that are not controlled.

1067
00:51:32,400 --> 00:51:35,400
Notably, in the current financial crisis, we

1068
00:51:35,400 --> 00:51:38,400
saw a huge drop in home values.

1069
00:51:38,400 --> 00:51:40,400
And we saw people who bought

1070
00:51:40,400 --> 00:51:42,400
homes at the top of the market,

1071
00:51:42,400 --> 00:51:44,400
and then they find that their mortgages

1072
00:51:44,400 --> 00:51:46,400
are worth more than their homes are.

1073
00:51:46,400 --> 00:51:49,400
And so, they have a negative net worth.

1074
00:51:49,400 --> 00:51:51,400
They're in trouble.

1075
00:51:51,400 --> 00:51:52,400
They would be bankrupt.

1076
00:51:52,400 --> 00:51:55,400
Maybe they're not bankrupt yet, but they're verging on that.

1077
00:51:55,400 --> 00:51:57,400
They're very unhappy.

1078
00:51:57,400 --> 00:52:00,400
This was a failure, I think,

1079
00:52:00,400 --> 00:52:03,400
of bringing finance to the people.

1080
00:52:03,400 --> 00:52:06,400
So, it's not democratizing finance.

1081
00:52:06,400 --> 00:52:12,400
We haven't finished democratizing finance.

1082
00:52:12,400 --> 00:52:13,900
So, you know, it's kind

1083
00:52:13,900 --> 00:52:15,400
of chaotic, the way things work,

1084
00:52:15,400 --> 00:52:18,400
for most people.

1085
00:52:18,400 --> 00:52:21,400
Most people who, A, do not have a lawyer.

1086
00:52:21,400 --> 00:52:24,400
B, do not have a financial advisor.

1087
00:52:24,400 --> 00:52:26,400
C, do not have an accountant.

1088
00:52:26,400 --> 00:52:27,400
All right?

1089
00:52:27,400 --> 00:52:29,900
Or maybe they go to

1090
00:52:29,900 --> 00:52:32,400
some, you know, storefront taxpaying service.

1091
00:52:32,400 --> 00:52:35,400
But that's as far as they go.

1092
00:52:35,400 --> 00:52:38,400
And these people make a mess of their lives.

1093
00:52:38,400 --> 00:52:41,400
So, for example, we have laws

1094
00:52:41,400 --> 00:52:44,400
that allow people to go bankrupt

1095
00:52:44,400 --> 00:52:48,400
and wipe off their debts.

1096
00:52:48,400 --> 00:52:50,400
All you have to do, if

1097
00:52:50,400 --> 00:52:52,400
you are in trouble, financial trouble,

1098
00:52:52,400 --> 00:52:55,400
is go to a lawyer and say,

1099
00:52:55,400 --> 00:52:58,400
can you help me file for Chapter 7 bankruptcy?

1100
00:52:58,400 --> 00:53:01,400
I'd like to wipe out all my debts.

1101
00:53:01,400 --> 00:53:02,900
But usually, you have to

1102
00:53:02,900 --> 00:53:04,400
have $1,000 to pay the lawyer

1103
00:53:04,400 --> 00:53:06,400
to help you do this.

1104
00:53:06,400 --> 00:53:09,400
And these people can't get it together to do that.

1105
00:53:09,400 --> 00:53:10,400
So, what happens?

1106
00:53:10,400 --> 00:53:13,400
What happens to this typical person who's uneducated,

1107
00:53:13,400 --> 00:53:15,400
has gotten deeply in debt?

1108
00:53:15,400 --> 00:53:17,400
What do you think happens?

1109
00:53:17,400 --> 00:53:19,400
Do they ever declare bankruptcy?

1110
00:53:19,400 --> 00:53:20,400
No.

1111
00:53:20,400 --> 00:53:21,400
What do they do?

1112
00:53:21,400 --> 00:53:24,400
They stop answering the phone, because they're getting these

1113
00:53:24,400 --> 00:53:27,400
dunning calls from creditors.

1114
00:53:27,400 --> 00:53:31,400
And so, the creditors then, it's called informal bankruptcy.

1115
00:53:31,400 --> 00:53:33,900
They will go to court and

1116
00:53:33,900 --> 00:53:36,400
ask the judge to allow them to

1117
00:53:36,400 --> 00:53:38,900
garnish the wages of the person who

1118
00:53:38,900 --> 00:53:41,400
won't pay and won't answer the phone.

1119
00:53:41,400 --> 00:53:43,400
So, they'll take another deduction from the person

1120
00:53:43,400 --> 00:53:46,400
who won't pay and the person's paycheck eventually,

1121
00:53:46,400 --> 00:53:48,400
the person never figures it out.

1122
00:53:48,400 --> 00:53:50,400
His paycheck just went down.

1123
00:53:50,400 --> 00:53:52,400
He's paying off his debt.

1124
00:53:52,400 --> 00:53:53,400
What a mess.

1125
00:53:53,400 --> 00:54:00,400
But that's because of the failure of financial institutions

1126
00:54:00,400 --> 00:54:03,400
to handle things well.

1127
00:54:03,400 --> 00:54:08,400
So, Elizabeth Warren, who is at, I mentioned her before,

1128
00:54:08,400 --> 00:54:10,400
at the Harvard Law School,

1129
00:54:10,400 --> 00:54:12,400
has written a couple of books

1130
00:54:12,400 --> 00:54:15,400
about the problems that people face.

1131
00:54:15,400 --> 00:54:19,400
And it's a testimony to the success of our democratic

1132
00:54:19,400 --> 00:54:21,900
government that she managed to

1133
00:54:21,900 --> 00:54:24,400
persuade Dodd and Frank to put

1134
00:54:24,400 --> 00:54:26,400
it in her bill, in their bill.

1135
00:54:30,400 --> 00:54:39,400
The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, which would

1136
00:54:39,400 --> 00:54:42,400
create a government agency that would try to limit the

1137
00:54:42,400 --> 00:54:50,400
abuse of lower-income, less-educated people.

1138
00:54:50,400 --> 00:54:52,400
We were hoping that she would

1139
00:54:52,400 --> 00:54:54,400
be made head of her bureau,

1140
00:54:54,400 --> 00:54:56,900
but it turns out that she

1141
00:54:56,900 --> 00:54:59,400
is only acting, I don't know what

1142
00:54:59,400 --> 00:55:02,400
her exact title is, transitioning into finding head

1143
00:55:02,400 --> 00:55:06,400
for the bureau, because the lobbyists that I told you

1144
00:55:06,400 --> 00:55:10,400
about representing credit card or mortgage industries are

1145
00:55:10,400 --> 00:55:12,400
adamantly opposed to seeing her.

1146
00:55:12,400 --> 00:55:14,900
They're not seeing her put

1147
00:55:14,900 --> 00:55:17,400
on as head of the bureau.

1148
00:55:17,400 --> 00:55:19,400
So, it looks like it's politically impossible to put

1149
00:55:19,400 --> 00:55:20,400
her in charge of it.

1150
00:55:20,400 --> 00:55:22,400
But she's at least involved

1151
00:55:22,400 --> 00:55:24,400
in helping pick the person who

1152
00:55:24,400 --> 00:55:26,400
would make that happen.

1153
00:55:26,400 --> 00:55:31,400
So, I think these are nice steps forward.

1154
00:55:31,400 --> 00:55:34,900
But I wrote a book, let me

1155
00:55:34,900 --> 00:55:38,400
just mention my own book, in 2008,

1156
00:55:38,400 --> 00:55:40,400
called Subprime Solution.

1157
00:55:42,400 --> 00:55:49,400
And so, I was trying to think of the future.

1158
00:55:49,400 --> 00:55:53,400
Again, I'm trying to think creatively and expansively

1159
00:55:53,400 --> 00:56:00,400
without thinking punitively, as Elizabeth Warren seems often

1160
00:56:00,400 --> 00:56:01,400
to do.

1161
00:56:01,400 --> 00:56:03,900
Her view tends to be that

1162
00:56:03,900 --> 00:56:06,400
there are exploiters who need to be

1163
00:56:06,400 --> 00:56:07,400
regulated.

1164
00:56:07,400 --> 00:56:10,400
But I'm thinking that maybe there's something positive we

1165
00:56:10,400 --> 00:56:11,400
can do.

1166
00:56:11,400 --> 00:56:13,400
So, I have various ideas.

1167
00:56:13,400 --> 00:56:16,900
Also, I had, this was 2008,

1168
00:56:16,900 --> 00:56:20,400
I also had another book, New

1169
00:56:20,400 --> 00:56:27,400
Financial Order in 2003.

1170
00:56:27,400 --> 00:56:30,400
I'm getting on toward 10 books now in my life.

1171
00:56:30,400 --> 00:56:32,400
I'm having trouble remembering which one is which.

1172
00:56:32,400 --> 00:56:34,400
I was just commenting to my wife.

1173
00:56:34,400 --> 00:56:36,400
It's a little bit of a problem.

1174
00:56:36,400 --> 00:56:38,400
But somewhere in these books, one

1175
00:56:38,400 --> 00:56:40,400
of the ideas I had actually

1176
00:56:40,400 --> 00:56:47,400
it's in New Financial Order, is for livelihood insurance.

1177
00:56:47,400 --> 00:56:49,400
That would help protect, this

1178
00:56:49,400 --> 00:56:51,400
is a financial institution that

1179
00:56:51,400 --> 00:56:53,400
would protect people's livelihoods.

1180
00:56:53,400 --> 00:56:55,400
I viewed it as an expansion

1181
00:56:55,400 --> 00:56:57,400
of something that we got already

1182
00:56:57,400 --> 00:56:59,400
called disability insurance.

1183
00:56:59,400 --> 00:57:00,400
In fact, it's been offered

1184
00:57:00,400 --> 00:57:01,400
by the government in the United

1185
00:57:01,400 --> 00:57:04,400
States as part of the Social Security system.

1186
00:57:04,400 --> 00:57:06,400
But it handles certain

1187
00:57:06,400 --> 00:57:08,400
insurance against certain specific kinds

1188
00:57:08,400 --> 00:57:09,400
of risks to livelihoods.

1189
00:57:09,400 --> 00:57:11,400
Namely, health risks.

1190
00:57:11,400 --> 00:57:13,400
If you become paralyzed, if

1191
00:57:13,400 --> 00:57:15,400
you become mentally ill, any of

1192
00:57:15,400 --> 00:57:17,900
those things that a doctor

1193
00:57:17,900 --> 00:57:20,400
can attest to is insured already.

1194
00:57:20,400 --> 00:57:22,400
Very important, because things like

1195
00:57:22,400 --> 00:57:24,400
that happen to people, and

1196
00:57:24,400 --> 00:57:26,400
they can't earn a living anymore.

1197
00:57:26,400 --> 00:57:28,400
And it happens to young people.

1198
00:57:28,400 --> 00:57:30,400
And so, we have private insurance.

1199
00:57:30,400 --> 00:57:34,400
The government has taken over part of disability insurance,

1200
00:57:34,400 --> 00:57:37,400
but there's also private disability insurance.

1201
00:57:37,400 --> 00:57:39,400
But none of this covers the biggest threats to people's

1202
00:57:39,400 --> 00:57:40,400
livelihoods.

1203
00:57:40,400 --> 00:57:46,400
Most threats to livelihoods are not due to medical events.

1204
00:57:46,400 --> 00:57:48,400
It's economic events that make

1205
00:57:48,400 --> 00:57:50,400
you, you know, you're 40 years

1206
00:57:50,400 --> 00:57:52,900
old, you've trained for,

1207
00:57:52,900 --> 00:57:55,400
let's say, nuclear engineering, okay?

1208
00:57:55,400 --> 00:58:00,400
And then we have the Fukushima or the Sendai disasters.

1209
00:58:00,400 --> 00:58:01,400
And then suddenly, no government

1210
00:58:01,400 --> 00:58:02,400
of the world wants to build

1211
00:58:02,400 --> 00:58:04,400
nuclear plants anymore.

1212
00:58:04,400 --> 00:58:05,900
So, here you are, you're

1213
00:58:05,900 --> 00:58:07,400
40 years old, you're reaching your

1214
00:58:07,400 --> 00:58:08,400
prime.

1215
00:58:08,400 --> 00:58:09,900
You would normally be making a

1216
00:58:09,900 --> 00:58:11,400
good high income, but now it's

1217
00:58:11,400 --> 00:58:12,400
useless.

1218
00:58:12,400 --> 00:58:14,400
No fault of your own.

1219
00:58:14,400 --> 00:58:16,400
This is a risk that you cannot

1220
00:58:16,400 --> 00:58:18,400
now insure, and it's part of the

1221
00:58:18,400 --> 00:58:20,400
thing that contributes to inequality.

1222
00:58:20,400 --> 00:58:23,400
And so, I think that we can insure those things.

1223
00:58:23,400 --> 00:58:25,400
And in the future, as finance

1224
00:58:25,400 --> 00:58:27,400
develops, these are some of the

1225
00:58:27,400 --> 00:58:30,400
missions that we have to do.

1226
00:58:30,400 --> 00:58:35,400
Another thing is home equity insurance that I mentioned

1227
00:58:35,400 --> 00:58:36,400
before, that the credit card.

1228
00:58:36,400 --> 00:58:38,400
I mentioned before that the

1229
00:58:38,400 --> 00:58:40,400
crisis was caused by a failure

1230
00:58:40,400 --> 00:58:44,400
to insure against home price risk.

1231
00:58:44,400 --> 00:58:48,400
I've been working on trying to get home equity insurance

1232
00:58:48,400 --> 00:58:49,400
started.

1233
00:58:49,400 --> 00:58:52,400
Some of my colleagues at

1234
00:58:52,400 --> 00:58:55,400
Yale, Will Goetzman and Barry Nailbuff

1235
00:58:55,400 --> 00:59:00,400
particularly, have actually created an insurance policy that

1236
00:59:00,400 --> 00:59:04,400
would insure homes against price declines in the city of

1237
00:59:04,400 --> 00:59:05,400
Syracuse, New York.

1238
00:59:05,400 --> 00:59:06,900
But it didn't really take off,

1239
00:59:06,900 --> 00:59:08,400
so this is still not happening

1240
00:59:08,400 --> 00:59:10,400
yet.

1241
00:59:10,400 --> 00:59:11,400
But here's the idea.

1242
00:59:11,400 --> 00:59:15,400
You can buy insurance against your home burning down.

1243
00:59:15,400 --> 00:59:18,400
That goes back 300 years.

1244
00:59:18,400 --> 00:59:20,400
But how often do homes burn down?

1245
00:59:20,400 --> 00:59:21,400
Not very often.

1246
00:59:21,400 --> 00:59:23,400
What's the real risk that you face?

1247
00:59:23,400 --> 00:59:27,400
It's the loss of economic value of a home.

1248
00:59:27,400 --> 00:59:34,400
And so, that is not insured anywhere in the world.

1249
00:59:34,400 --> 00:59:35,400
Why not?

1250
00:59:35,400 --> 00:59:36,400
We could insure it.

1251
00:59:36,400 --> 00:59:40,400
I think these are things that would, developing home equity

1252
00:59:40,400 --> 00:59:44,400
insurance or livelihood insurance would be positive

1253
00:59:44,400 --> 00:59:45,400
steps.

1254
00:59:45,400 --> 00:59:48,400
I have one more example from this book, Subprime Solution.

1255
00:59:48,400 --> 00:59:59,400
Something I call a continuous workout mortgage.

1256
00:59:59,400 --> 01:00:00,400
Okay.

1257
01:00:00,400 --> 01:00:01,900
In the financial crisis today,

1258
01:00:01,900 --> 01:00:03,400
right now, there's a lot of

1259
01:00:03,400 --> 01:00:04,900
people who are on the

1260
01:00:04,900 --> 01:00:06,400
verge of defaulting on their mortgages.

1261
01:00:06,400 --> 01:00:09,400
Right now, there are 2.5 million households that are on

1262
01:00:09,400 --> 01:00:12,400
the verge of defaulting on their mortgages.

1263
01:00:12,400 --> 01:00:13,900
Haven't yet, but they are

1264
01:00:13,900 --> 01:00:15,400
at risk of defaulting and being

1265
01:00:15,400 --> 01:00:17,400
thrown out of their houses.

1266
01:00:17,400 --> 01:00:20,400
So, that's something like close to 10 million people.

1267
01:00:20,400 --> 01:00:22,400
Big time event.

1268
01:00:22,400 --> 01:00:24,400
Why is it that they're being thrown out?

1269
01:00:24,400 --> 01:00:26,400
Well, because their home value has dropped.

1270
01:00:26,400 --> 01:00:28,400
Maybe they're unemployed, their income has dropped.

1271
01:00:28,400 --> 01:00:31,400
We still have 8.8% unemployment.

1272
01:00:31,400 --> 01:00:32,400
And they can't pay their mortgage.

1273
01:00:32,400 --> 01:00:36,400
So, they think it's futile, because they're paying a debt

1274
01:00:36,400 --> 01:00:38,400
that is bigger than their wealth.

1275
01:00:38,400 --> 01:00:40,400
And so, maybe they don't feel

1276
01:00:40,400 --> 01:00:42,400
in a very good mood about it.

1277
01:00:42,400 --> 01:00:43,900
They go back to the mortgage

1278
01:00:43,900 --> 01:00:45,400
lender and ask for a workout.

1279
01:00:45,400 --> 01:00:48,400
And the mortgage lender typically says no.

1280
01:00:48,400 --> 01:00:50,400
The government has done a

1281
01:00:50,400 --> 01:00:52,400
sequence of programs to try to

1282
01:00:52,400 --> 01:00:57,400
encourage the servicers of mortgages to do workouts.

1283
01:00:57,400 --> 01:01:00,400
That means lower their payments or somehow make it easier.

1284
01:01:00,400 --> 01:01:02,400
But it's been disappointing.

1285
01:01:02,400 --> 01:01:04,400
They haven't succeeded in

1286
01:01:04,400 --> 01:01:06,400
getting cooperation on these programs.

1287
01:01:06,400 --> 01:01:10,400
It's one of the big tragedies of the financial crisis.

1288
01:01:10,400 --> 01:01:14,400
So, what I propose is that we should think forward.

1289
01:01:14,400 --> 01:01:15,400
I don't know how we

1290
01:01:15,400 --> 01:01:16,400
can solve this mess right now.

1291
01:01:16,400 --> 01:01:19,400
But think about in the future

1292
01:01:19,400 --> 01:01:22,400
having mortgages that have a pre-planned workout.

1293
01:01:22,400 --> 01:01:30,400
And the workout would lower the cost of,

1294
01:01:30,400 --> 01:01:36,400
lower the payment on the mortgage continuously.

1295
01:01:36,400 --> 01:01:38,400
The other problem with workouts is,

1296
01:01:38,400 --> 01:01:41,400
even when people get a workout on their mortgage,

1297
01:01:41,400 --> 01:01:44,400
they default anyway, because things get even worse later.

1298
01:01:44,400 --> 01:01:46,400
You've got one workout, you go back and say,

1299
01:01:46,400 --> 01:01:48,400
I'd like another workout.

1300
01:01:48,400 --> 01:01:50,400
They say, you've got to be kidding.

1301
01:01:50,400 --> 01:01:53,400
Anyway, the government, like the HAMP program that

1302
01:01:53,400 --> 01:01:58,400
the Obama administration has promoted,

1303
01:01:58,400 --> 01:02:00,400
has only one workout for each.

1304
01:02:00,400 --> 01:02:02,400
And that's for families.

1305
01:02:02,400 --> 01:02:05,400
So, I think they should be continuous and automatic.

1306
01:02:05,400 --> 01:02:14,400
And they don't require anyone to apply for a workout.

1307
01:02:14,400 --> 01:02:17,400
Okay.

1308
01:02:17,400 --> 01:02:19,400
So, how much time do I have?

1309
01:02:19,400 --> 01:02:21,400
I think I'll move to my last subject,

1310
01:02:21,400 --> 01:02:25,400
which is your career.

1311
01:02:25,400 --> 01:02:29,400
Because you are young people.

1312
01:02:29,400 --> 01:02:33,400
And you may be wondering what you want to do.

1313
01:02:33,400 --> 01:02:40,400
So, I think that,

1314
01:02:40,400 --> 01:02:43,400
I think maybe I've reflected on this before.

1315
01:02:43,400 --> 01:02:47,400
You probably feel that you want to do something important.

1316
01:02:47,400 --> 01:02:52,400
And you want a sort of perfect career.

1317
01:02:52,400 --> 01:02:55,400
Something that tells a story,

1318
01:02:55,400 --> 01:02:58,400
makes a story of your life.

1319
01:02:58,400 --> 01:03:05,400
And ultimately serves for good causes.

1320
01:03:05,400 --> 01:03:11,400
But it's, when you read Unger,

1321
01:03:11,400 --> 01:03:14,400
you don't get an inspiration like that, right?

1322
01:03:14,400 --> 01:03:16,400
You get, he says, write

1323
01:03:16,400 --> 01:03:18,400
a check right now to UNICEF.

1324
01:03:18,400 --> 01:03:19,400
Well, I can do that.

1325
01:03:19,400 --> 01:03:22,400
But does that, you know, it seems unrewarding,

1326
01:03:22,400 --> 01:03:24,400
just to give to charity.

1327
01:03:24,400 --> 01:03:26,400
I can just live like a monk, right?

1328
01:03:26,400 --> 01:03:31,400
I could take a job at a hamburger joint, right?

1329
01:03:31,400 --> 01:03:34,400
And then give all my money away to UNICEF.

1330
01:03:34,400 --> 01:03:36,400
Some of that doesn't feel right.

1331
01:03:36,400 --> 01:03:38,400
Because I think that, you know,

1332
01:03:38,400 --> 01:03:40,400
you know that you have abilities.

1333
01:03:40,400 --> 01:03:42,400
And you want to see them flourish.

1334
01:03:42,400 --> 01:03:45,400
And you want to, that's why I think,

1335
01:03:45,400 --> 01:03:47,400
you shouldn't be flipping hamburgers

1336
01:03:47,400 --> 01:03:48,400
and giving the money away.

1337
01:03:48,400 --> 01:03:51,400
That's not what you should be doing now.

1338
01:03:51,400 --> 01:03:53,400
And instead, it would be learning things

1339
01:03:53,400 --> 01:03:55,400
that make it possible

1340
01:03:55,400 --> 01:03:59,400
to do good works.

1341
01:03:59,400 --> 01:04:03,400
So, I don't know what,

1342
01:04:03,400 --> 01:04:05,400
what is the perfect career?

1343
01:04:05,400 --> 01:04:10,400
I mean, I mentioned Paul Allen or Bill Gates.

1344
01:04:10,400 --> 01:04:13,400
Bill Gates, I shouldn't give you an example

1345
01:04:13,400 --> 01:04:14,400
of dropping out of college,

1346
01:04:14,400 --> 01:04:16,400
but he dropped out of college.

1347
01:04:16,400 --> 01:04:18,400
And by the way, you should do that

1348
01:04:18,400 --> 01:04:22,400
if you have a Microsoft-size idea.

1349
01:04:22,400 --> 01:04:24,400
But I think I've said this before.

1350
01:04:24,400 --> 01:04:25,400
We took,

1351
01:04:25,400 --> 01:04:27,400
we took a lot of time to do this.

1352
01:04:27,400 --> 01:04:28,400
But,

1353
01:04:34,402 --> 01:04:34,662
